---
// Diviniti case study — "Redefining Design". Figma: "Diviniti — Fixed" (362:391), 1728 × 11621, page fill #fcfbf8,
// one accent with the rest of the site. Blocks sit at their design coordinates (styles/case.css); the order is reading order for phones.
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Nav from '../../components/Nav.astro';
import Footer from '../../components/Footer.astro';
import NextProject from '../../components/NextProject.astro';
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import '../../styles/case.css';

import hero from '../../assets/home/diviniti-cover.png';   // the same picture as the home page's card, so the card grows into this hero
import logoMask from '../../assets/diviniti/logo-mask.png';
import definition from '../../assets/diviniti/definition.png';
import pixels from '../../assets/diviniti/pixels.png';
import earlyWide from '../../assets/diviniti/early-wide.jpg';
import early1 from '../../assets/diviniti/early-1.jpg';
import early2 from '../../assets/diviniti/early-2.jpg';
import screens from '../../assets/diviniti/screens.png';
import portraitOnGold from '../../assets/diviniti/portrait-on-gold.png';
import productCard from '../../assets/diviniti/product-card.png';
import ar from '../../assets/diviniti/ar.png';
import intelligence from '../../assets/diviniti/intelligence.png';

const half = '(max-width: 900px) 100vw, 852px';
---
<Base title="A year that took my design from pixels to platforms — Diviniti · Suyash" description="A year at Diviniti India, from lighting product images by hand to the product data behind the site, its marketplaces and its AI.">
  <Nav solid />
  <main class="canvas diviniti-page" style="height: calc(12060 * var(--u));">

    <!-- ===== Hero (Diviniti Global 362:392) ===== -->
    <header class="bleed hero" style="--y: 0; --h: 1117; view-transition-name: vt-diviniti;">
      <Image class="hero__img" src={hero} alt="A silver and gold Ganesha idol beside a white flower on a dark wooden table" widths={[1440, 1728]} sizes="100vw" loading="eager" />
      <span class="hero__shade" aria-hidden="true"></span>
    </header>
    <span class="at hero__logo" style={`--x: 104; --y: 145; --w: 131.664; --h: 130.263; --mask: url('${logoMask.src}');`} aria-label="Diviniti" role="img"></span>
    <!-- the hero's words are one block that stands on the hero's foot (56 design px above it) and grows upwards, so text
         that wraps to more lines at smaller windows never runs out of the picture -->
    <div class="at hero__block" style="--x: 104; --y: 1061;">
    <p class="hero__kicker hero__text" data-hero-in>Diviniti India · Redefining design · 2025–26</p>
    <h1 class="hero__title hero__text" data-hero-in>A year that took my design from <span>pixels to platforms</span></h1>
    <!-- the lede and its facts flow as one block, so the facts always sit below the lede however many lines it wraps to -->
    <div class="hero__intro">
      <p class="c-lead hero__text" data-hero-in>From lighting product images by hand to the product data behind the site, its marketplaces and its AI.</p>
      <dl class="hero__facts" data-hero-in>
        <div><dt>Role</dt><dd>Design intern, then Founder’s Office</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Disciplines</dt><dd>Product design · Visual design · E&#8209;commerce · AI</dd></div>
      </dl>
    </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ===== Prologue: a different definition (362:408, y 1117) ===== -->
    <div class="at stack wide-mid" style="--x: 104; --y: 1277; --w: 491;" data-slide="left">
      <h2 class="c-h2" data-n="Prologue" style="margin-bottom: calc(32 * var(--u));">A different definition</h2>
      <div class="c-body">
        <p>When I joined Diviniti, I thought I understood what design was. To me, good design meant creating beautiful visuals: the kind that captured attention, looked polished, and felt premium.</p>
        <p>That belief wasn’t unusual. Most of my experience until then had been shaped by screens, layouts, typography, and imagery. Design was something you could see. Diviniti challenged that assumption almost immediately.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="at c-body wide-mid" style="--x: 876; --y: 1368; --w: 491;" data-slide="right" data-delay="0.12">
      <p>The further I went, the less design seemed to be about creating artifacts. It became a way of understanding how information moves, how decisions are made, how systems connect, and how people experience a business as a whole.</p>
      <p>This journey is not about a website launch, a brand redesign, or a single product. It is about how my definition of design evolved. Over the following year, I worked across websites, luxury brands, AI systems, ecommerce operations, product data, and internal business processes. With every project, the problems became less visual and more structural.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="bleed c-img" style="--y: 1792; --h: 558;" data-zoom="pass">
      <Image src={definition} alt="A desk with notebooks and objects in warm, low light" widths={[1024]} sizes="100vw" data-parallax="6" />
    </div>

    <!-- ===== The year in six steps: an index that is also the argument. Each step sits further in than the last,
         from what people see to what they never see, and links to its chapter. ===== -->
    <nav class="at ladder" style="--x: 104; --y: 2490; --w: 1520;" aria-label="The six chapters" data-slide="left">
      <p class="ladder__head"><span>Six steps, each one further from the surface</span><span>What people see</span></p>
      <ol>
        <li style="--i: 0;"><a href="#pixels"><span class="ladder__n">01</span><span class="ladder__name">Pixels</span><span class="ladder__line">Design influences perception.</span><span class="ladder__made c-body">Product visuals, composed and lit by hand</span></a></li>
        <li style="--i: 1;"><a href="#screens"><span class="ladder__n">02</span><span class="ladder__name">Screens</span><span class="ladder__line">Design influences decisions.</span><span class="ladder__made c-body">Portrait on Gold: the site, its checkout, the AR preview</span></a></li>
        <li style="--i: 2;"><a href="#intelligence"><span class="ladder__n">03</span><span class="ladder__name">Intelligence</span><span class="ladder__line">Design influences interactions.</span><span class="ladder__made c-body">Amy, a chatbot for customer enquiries</span></a></li>
        <li style="--i: 3;"><a href="#systems"><span class="ladder__n">04</span><span class="ladder__name">Systems</span><span class="ladder__line">Design influences organizations.</span><span class="ladder__made c-body">How orders, manufacturing and support connect</span></a></li>
        <li style="--i: 4;"><a href="#data"><span class="ladder__n">05</span><span class="ladder__name">Data</span><span class="ladder__line">Design influences information.</span><span class="ladder__made c-body">Product data, taxonomy and SKU architecture</span></a></li>
        <li style="--i: 5;"><a href="#platforms"><span class="ladder__n">06</span><span class="ladder__name">Platforms</span><span class="ladder__line">Design influences systems.</span><span class="ladder__made c-body">One product database behind the site, marketplaces, tools and AI</span></a></li>
      </ol>
      <p class="ladder__foot">What people never see</p>
    </nav>

    <!-- ===== 01 Pixels (362:413, y 2350) ===== -->
    <div class="at stack" style="--x: 104; --y: 3756; --w: 491;" data-slide="left">
      <h2 class="c-h2" id="pixels" data-n="01" style="margin-bottom: calc(16 * var(--u));">Pixels</h2>
      <h3 class="c-h3" style="margin-bottom: calc(24 * var(--u));">Design influences perception.</h3>
      <div class="c-body">
        <p>My first months at Diviniti were spent creating visuals. Not websites, not products: just images. I became obsessed with details that most people never consciously notice. Light. Materials. Texture. Composition.</p>
        <p>This was mid-2025, before generative AI could depict a product accurately, so every element and every light source had to be placed by hand. The surprising part was how dramatically those details changed the way a product was perceived.</p>
        <p>The product remained the same. Only the presentation changed. That was my first lesson:</p>
      </div>
      <p class="c-quote" style="margin-top: calc(24 * var(--u));">Perception is designed.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="at c-img img-bottom" style="--x: 876; --y: 3410; --w: 852; --h: 1117;" data-wipe="right">
      <Image src={pixels} alt="Golden idols rendered on a dark, lit set" widths={[852, 901]} sizes={half} />
    </div>

    <!-- ===== Early works (362:418, y 3467, h 1296, dark gradient) ===== -->
    <section class="bleed early" style="--y: 4527; --h: 1296;" aria-label="Early works"></section>
    <div class="at c-img" style="--x: 104; --y: 4687; --w: 1520; --h: 608;" data-wipe="left"><Image src={earlyWide} alt="999 Silver — crafted for legacy: a gold idol on a cream backdrop" widths={[1280]} sizes="(max-width: 900px) 100vw, 1520px" /></div>
    <h3 class="at early__title c-h3" style="--x: 104; --y: 5343; --w: 491;" data-slide="left">Early works</h3>
    <div class="at c-body early__copy" style="--x: 104; --y: 5389; --w: 491;" data-slide="left">
      <p>In mid-2025, generative AI couldn’t depict a product accurately, so I built every element of these images by hand: the walls, lights, shadows and reflections you see here.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="at c-img" style="--x: 960; --y: 5343; --w: 320; --h: 320;" data-wipe="right"><Image src={early1} alt="Silver figurines on a dark table" widths={[320, 640]} sizes="320px" /></div>
    <div class="at c-img" style="--x: 1304; --y: 5343; --w: 320; --h: 320;" data-wipe="right"><Image src={early2} alt="A silver idol under a spotlight" widths={[320, 640]} sizes="320px" /></div>

    <!-- ===== 02 Screens (362:424, y 4763) ===== -->
    <div class="at stack" style="--x: 104; --y: 6161; --w: 491;" data-slide="left">
      <h2 class="c-h2" id="screens" data-n="02" style="margin-bottom: calc(16 * var(--u));">Screens</h2>
      <h3 class="c-h3" style="margin-bottom: calc(24 * var(--u));">Design influences decisions.</h3>
      <div class="c-body">
        <p>As my work expanded from visuals to ecommerce, I began seeing a different side of design. A beautiful interface could attract attention, but it couldn’t create trust. Customers needed context. They needed reassurance. They needed enough information to feel confident in a decision.</p>
        <p>The more I worked on websites and customer journeys, the more obvious this became. People weren’t evaluating pixels. They were evaluating risk. That changed the way I thought about design. It wasn’t just shaping perception anymore:</p>
      </div>
      <p class="c-quote" style="margin-top: calc(24 * var(--u));">it was helping people make decisions.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="at c-img" style="--x: 876; --y: 5823; --w: 852; --h: 1117;" data-wipe="right"><Image src={screens} alt="The Portrait on Gold website open on a laptop in a warm room" widths={[852]} sizes={half} /></div>

    <!-- ===== Portrait on Gold ===== -->
    <div class="at stack wide-mid" style="--x: 104; --y: 7100; --w: 491;" data-slide="left">
      <h3 class="c-h3" style="margin-bottom: calc(24 * var(--u));">Portrait on Gold</h3>
      <div class="c-body">
        <p>This is where my first real project comes in. Diviniti had more than 5,000 SKUs, in categories spread across spiritual and corporate gifting.</p>
        <p>Portrait on Gold was a completely different product, serving a completely different audience. The average piece costs ₹1 lakh (Diviniti’s average is ₹2,000). So to give it a home, Portrait on Gold™ was launched, and I was given the task of making it live, from its visual direction to the UI and UX of its website. It is live today and taking orders.</p>
      </div>
      <h4 class="c-sub">Visuals</h4>
      <div class="c-body">
        <p>The visuals had to be clean and show the product in settings that bring out emotion, enough to make you trust a ₹1 lakh product.</p>
        <p>The biggest thing I learned about was aspiration: how people want to be something better than they are today. Designing around that gave the highest return.</p>
      </div>
      <h4 class="c-sub">What I learned</h4>
      <div class="c-body">
        <p>Earlier I believed that websites revolved around visuals. By this time, I started to understand the real use of UX in design. To sell luxury, you can’t follow the guidelines you have seen till now. Luxury doesn’t shout: it is subtle, informative and authoritative, and the visuals, the UI and the UX should be worth the price the customer pays.</p>
        <p>It doesn’t optimize for speed like a SaaS product, and it doesn’t flood you with discounts like Amazon or Flipkart. It brings you in slowly, guides you, builds your trust, and helps you make the right decision.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="at c-img img-bottom" style="--x: 876; --y: 6940; --w: 852; --h: 1252;" data-wipe="right"><Image src={portraitOnGold} alt="The Portrait on Gold site on a phone: Life, framed in Gold" widths={[746]} sizes={half} /></div>

    <!-- ===== Product design ===== -->
    <div class="at stack wide-mid" style="--x: 104; --y: 8291; --w: 491;" data-slide="left">
      <h3 class="c-h3" style="margin-bottom: calc(24 * var(--u));">Product design</h3>
      <p class="c-body">This marked my first interaction with product design in a large organization. I learnt e-commerce design wasn’t just about pretty visuals on the landing page. It meant every page, every feature revolved around the product.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="at product-card" style="--x: 104; --y: 8514; --w: 1044; --h: 589;" data-wipe="left">
      <Image src={productCard} alt="The Classic Grace framed portrait product page, with a portrait preview and price" widths={[1102, 1716]} sizes="(max-width: 900px) 100vw, 1102px" />
    </div>
    <div class="at c-img ar" style="--x: 1196; --y: 8514; --w: 428; --h: 589;" data-wipe="right"><Image src={ar} alt="Previewing a framed portrait on a wall in augmented reality" widths={[428, 856]} sizes="(max-width: 900px) 100vw, 428px" /></div>
    <p class="at c-body caption" style="--x: 104; --y: 9127; --w: 491;" data-slide="left"><span>01</span>Checkout flow. A custom checkout lets customers upload their portrait and preview the finished piece before paying a single rupee. Live on the site.</p>
    <p class="at c-body caption" style="--x: 1196; --y: 9127; --w: 428;" data-slide="right" data-delay="0.24"><span>02</span>Augmented reality. Customers can place any product in their own room in AR before they buy. Shipped.</p>

    <!-- ===== 03 Intelligence (362:485, y 8299) ===== -->
    <div class="at stack" style="--x: 104; --y: 9697; --w: 491;" data-slide="left">
      <h2 class="c-h2" id="intelligence" data-n="03" style="margin-bottom: calc(16 * var(--u));">Intelligence</h2>
      <h3 class="c-h3" style="margin-bottom: calc(24 * var(--u));">Design influences interactions.</h3>
      <div class="c-body">
        <p>My introduction to AI began with a simple question: what happens when software can participate in the customer experience? I started experimenting with chatbots, automation, and AI-assisted workflows. Some worked. Some didn’t.</p>
        <p>What interested me wasn’t the technology itself. It was the role it played. The goal wasn’t to replace people; it was to remove friction. The more I worked with AI, the more I realized that technology is most valuable when it handles routine interactions and leaves meaningful moments to humans. A good experience wasn’t created by AI or people alone. It emerged from the way both worked together.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="at c-img" style="--x: 876; --y: 9359; --w: 852; --h: 1117;" data-wipe="right"><Image src={intelligence} alt="Chatbot Amy answering a customer on a phone" widths={[852, 1065]} sizes={half} /></div>
    <p class="at note c-body" style="--x: 1231; --y: 10312; --w: 393;" data-slide="right" data-delay="0.24">Chatbot Amy, built with Python and the OpenAI API. Live today, answering customer enquiries, shipment questions and product recommendations.</p>

    <!-- ===== 04 Systems · 05 Data · 06 Platforms (362:431, y 9416) ===== -->
    <div class="at stack" style="--x: 104; --y: 10636; --w: 448;" data-slide="left">
      <h2 class="c-h2" id="systems" data-n="04" style="margin-bottom: calc(16 * var(--u));">Systems</h2>
      <h3 class="c-h3" style="margin-bottom: calc(24 * var(--u));">Design influences organizations.</h3>
      <div class="c-body">
        <p>As I spent more time inside the business, I found myself looking beyond products and screens. I began understanding how orders were fulfilled, how products were manufactured, how customer support operated, and how decisions moved through the company. What surprised me was how connected everything was.</p>
        <p>A customer never experiences marketing, sales, operations, and support as separate functions. They experience one company. Every interaction is shaped by systems most customers never see. The further I looked, the harder it became to separate design from the organization itself.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="at stack" style="--x: 640; --y: 10636; --w: 448;" data-slide="right" data-delay="0.12">
      <h2 class="c-h2" id="data" data-n="05" style="margin-bottom: calc(16 * var(--u));">Data</h2>
      <h3 class="c-h3" style="margin-bottom: calc(24 * var(--u));">Design influences information.</h3>
      <div class="c-body">
        <p>The biggest shift in my thinking didn’t come from a website, a brand, or an AI tool. It came from product data. As Diviniti began preparing for AI-powered commerce, the conversation moved away from interfaces and towards information: product structures, databases, taxonomy, SKU architecture, and a single source of truth.</p>
        <p>At first, none of it felt like design. There were no screens to improve and no visuals to refine. Yet many of the problems we were trying to solve originated here: products that were difficult to find, information that was inconsistent, systems that couldn’t scale. Some of the most important design decisions never appear on a screen. They exist in the way information is organized.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="at stack" style="--x: 1176; --y: 10636; --w: 448;" data-slide="right" data-delay="0.24">
      <h2 class="c-h2" id="platforms" data-n="06" style="margin-bottom: calc(16 * var(--u));">Platforms</h2>
      <h3 class="c-h3" style="margin-bottom: calc(24 * var(--u));">Design influences systems.</h3>
      <div class="c-body">
        <p>By this point, the boundaries between design, technology, operations, and information had started to blur. The challenge was no longer creating a better page or a better flow, but systems that could support many experiences at once. A product database could power a website; the same information could feed marketplaces, internal tools, customer support, and increasingly, AI systems.</p>
        <p>The more connected these systems became, the less important individual interfaces felt. What mattered was the structure beneath them. The interface was only one expression of a much larger system.</p>
      </div>
      <p class="c-quote" style="margin-top: calc(24 * var(--u));">Design was no longer about creating artifacts. It was about creating foundations that could scale.</p>
    </div>

    <!-- ===== Epilogue (362:493, y 10226) ===== -->
    <div class="at stack" style="--x: 104; --y: 11446; --w: 491;" data-slide="left">
      <h2 class="c-h2" data-n="Epilogue" style="margin-bottom: calc(24 * var(--u));">Redefining design</h2>
      <div class="c-body">
        <p>When I joined Diviniti, I saw design as the act of creating: creating visuals, creating interfaces, creating experiences. Over time, that definition became difficult to sustain. The problems that shaped my thinking were rarely visual.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="at c-body" style="--x: 876; --y: 11528; --w: 491;" data-slide="right" data-delay="0.12">
      <p>They lived inside customer journeys, business operations, product structures, and information systems. The further I moved from the surface, the more I realized that design was never limited to what people see. It exists in the decisions that shape how people, products, information, and technology work together.</p>
      <p>This journey began with pixels. It ended with systems.</p>
    </div>
    <p class="at c-quote closing" style="--x: 104; --y: 11800; --w: 760;" data-slide="left">Design did not become bigger.<br />My definition of it did.</p>
    <p class="at c-body now" style="--x: 104; --y: 11960; --w: 491;" data-slide="left">I joined Diviniti as a design intern in May 2025. Since August 2026 I work in its Founder’s Office.</p>
  </main>
  <NextProject href="/work/falcon" label="FALCON" title="Making millions of network events explain a single outage" />
  <Footer />
</Base>

<script>
  import '../../scripts/case-motion';
</script>

<style>
  /* a column of text that flows: headings and paragraphs push each other down instead of sitting at fixed heights */
  .stack { display: flex; flex-direction: column; }
  /* mid-size windows: text keeps a readable minimum size, so these columns use the free space before the image */
  @media (min-width: 901px) and (max-width: 1365px) { .wide-mid { width: calc(740 * var(--u)) !important; } }


  /* a small serif sub-heading inside a column (Swad's h3) */
  .c-sub { margin: calc(40 * var(--u)) 0 calc(10 * var(--u)); font-family: var(--serif); font-weight: 300; font-variation-settings: "SOFT" 0, "WONK" 1;
    font-size: max(17px, calc(22 * var(--u))); line-height: 1.3; }
  /* a numbered note under a picture: its number in mono accent, as Swad's figure numbers */
  .caption span { display: block; margin-bottom: .4em; font-family: var(--mono); font-size: max(11px, calc(13 * var(--u))); letter-spacing: .08em; color: var(--accent); }

  /* ---- hero ---- */
  .hero { overflow: hidden; background: var(--night); }
  .hero :global(.hero__img) { width: 100%; height: 100%; object-fit: cover; }
  .hero__shade { position: absolute; left: calc((var(--vw, 100vw) - 1728 * var(--u)) / 2); top: calc(19 * var(--u)); width: calc(985 * var(--u)); height: calc(1098 * var(--u));
    background: linear-gradient(to left, rgba(12, 11, 9, 0), rgba(14, 13, 10, 0) 31.25%, var(--night)); }
  .hero__text { color: var(--paper); }
  @media (min-width: 901px) {
    .hero__block { translate: 0 -100%; }
    .hero__block .hero__title { width: calc(760 * var(--u)); margin-top: calc(20 * var(--u)); }
    .hero__intro { width: max(calc(507 * var(--u)), 420px); margin-top: calc(56 * var(--u)); }
  }
  .hero__logo { background: var(--paper); -webkit-mask: var(--mask) center / 100% 100% no-repeat; mask: var(--mask) center / 100% 100% no-repeat; }
  .hero__title { font-family: var(--serif); font-weight: 300; font-style: italic; font-variation-settings: "SOFT" 0, "WONK" 1;
    font-size: calc(61 * var(--u)); line-height: 1.15; text-wrap: balance; }
  .hero__title span { color: var(--accent-on-dark); }
  .hero__kicker { font-family: var(--mono); font-size: max(11px, calc(13 * var(--u))); letter-spacing: .08em; text-transform: uppercase; opacity: .8; }
  /* the facts as in Swad's hero: a mono label over each */
  .hero__facts { display: grid; grid-template-columns: calc(174 * var(--u)) minmax(0, 1fr); column-gap: calc(33 * var(--u)); margin: calc(36 * var(--u)) 0 0;
    padding-top: calc(24 * var(--u)); border-top: 1px solid rgba(252, 251, 248, .3); color: var(--paper); }
  .hero__facts dt { font-family: var(--mono); font-size: max(11px, calc(13 * var(--u))); letter-spacing: .08em; text-transform: uppercase; opacity: .7; }
  .hero__facts dd { margin: calc(6 * var(--u)) 0 0; font-size: max(13px, calc(16 * var(--u))); line-height: 1.5; }

  /* ---- sections ---- */
  .img-bottom :global(img) { object-position: bottom; }
  .early { background: linear-gradient(61.59deg, #1d0d03 3.764%, #26130a 67.816%, #3b1f12 103.19%); }
  .early__title { color: var(--paper); }
  .early__copy { color: var(--paper); }
  .product-card { overflow: hidden; box-shadow: var(--shadow-big); }
  .product-card :global(img) { position: absolute; left: 2.84%; top: 7.37%; width: 105.58%; height: 100%; max-width: none; }
  .ar { border-radius: var(--radius); box-shadow: var(--shadow-big); }
  .note { background: var(--paper); padding: calc(30 * var(--u)); filter: var(--drop-big, drop-shadow(-20px 20px 13px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.08))); display: flex; align-items: center; }
  .closing { font-size: max(22px, calc(31 * var(--u))); line-height: 1.25; }
  .now { color: var(--muted); padding-top: calc(16 * var(--u)); border-top: 1px solid var(--muted); }

  /* ---- the six steps: a staircase of hairlines, each row starting further in ---- */
  .ladder ol { list-style: none; margin: calc(18 * var(--u)) 0 0; padding: 0; }
  .ladder__head { display: flex; justify-content: space-between; color: var(--muted); text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: .08em; }
  .ladder__head, .ladder__foot { font-family: var(--mono); font-size: max(11px, calc(13 * var(--u))); }
  .ladder li { margin-left: calc(var(--i) * 96 * var(--u)); border-top: 1px solid var(--ink); }
